# Semantic HTML & Accessibility

HTML is not only about making things appear on a webpage.

HTML should also tell the browser **what each part of the page actually means**.

For example, instead of using:

```html
<div>My Blog</div>
```

we can use:

```html
<header>My Blog</header>
```

The second one gives the content a meaningful purpose.

This is called **Semantic HTML**.

---

# 1. What Does "Semantic" Mean?

Semantic basically means:

> **Using an HTML element according to what its content means.**

For example:

```html
<button>Submit</button>
```

means:

> This is something the user can click as a button.

While:

```html
<div>Submit</div>
```

only means:

> This is a generic container containing the word "Submit".

The browser and assistive technologies can understand the first one much better.

---

# 2. Semantic vs Non-Semantic HTML

### Non-semantic

```html
<div class="header">
    My Website
</div>

<div class="content">
    Hello everyone!
</div>

<div class="footer">
    Copyright 2026
</div>
```

Everything is a generic `<div>`.

### Semantic

```html
<header>
    My Website
</header>

<main>
    Hello everyone!
</main>

<footer>
    Copyright 2026
</footer>
```

Now the structure communicates meaning.

---

# 3. Important Semantic Elements

Some commonly used semantic elements are:

```text
<header>
<nav>
<main>
<section>
<article>
<aside>
<footer>
```

Each represents a different type of content.

---

# 4. `<header>`

`<header>` represents introductory content for a page or section.

Example:

```html
<header>
    <h1>My Blog</h1>
    <p>Welcome to my blog.</p>
</header>
```

A header can contain things like:

- Logo
- Heading
- Introduction
- Navigation

---

# 5. `<nav>`

`<nav>` represents navigation links.

```html
<nav>
    <a href="/">Home</a>
    <a href="/about">About</a>
    <a href="/contact">Contact</a>
</nav>
```

Instead of:

```html
<div class="navigation">
```

use:

```html
<nav>
```

when the content represents navigation.

---

# 6. `<main>`

`<main>` contains the **main content of the page**.

```html
<main>
    <h1>My Portfolio</h1>

    <p>
        I am a Computer Science student.
    </p>
</main>
```

The main content should be distinguishable from things like navigation, headers, and footers.

---

# 7. `<section>`

`<section>` represents a meaningful section of content.

Example:

```html
<section>
    <h2>My Projects</h2>

    <p>
        Here are some of my projects.
    </p>
</section>
```

A section normally has a heading that explains what the section is about.

---

# 8. `<article>`

`<article>` is useful for content that can stand on its own.

Examples:

- Blog post
- News article
- Forum post
- Product review

Example:

```html
<article>
    <h2>Understanding JavaScript</h2>

    <p>
        JavaScript is a programming language used
        to make web pages interactive.
    </p>
</article>
```

The article could theoretically be separated from the rest of the page and still make sense.

---

# 9. `<aside>`

`<aside>` contains content related to the main content but not part of its central flow.

Example:

```html
<aside>
    <h2>Related Articles</h2>

    <a href="#">JavaScript Basics</a>
    <a href="#">DOM Tutorial</a>
</aside>
```

Common uses:

- Sidebars
- Related content
- Additional information
- Advertisements

---

# 10. `<footer>`

`<footer>` represents footer information.

```html
<footer>
    <p>© 2026 Nikitha</p>
</footer>
```

It can contain:

- Copyright information
- Contact information
- Additional links
- Author information

---

# 11. Complete Semantic Structure

A simple webpage might look like:

```html
<body>

    <header>
        <h1>My Website</h1>

        <nav>
            <a href="#">Home</a>
            <a href="#">Projects</a>
            <a href="#">Contact</a>
        </nav>
    </header>

    <main>

        <section>
            <h2>About Me</h2>
            <p>Computer Science student.</p>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2>Projects</h2>

            <article>
                <h3>Expense Tracker</h3>
                <p>A simple web application.</p>
            </article>
        </section>

    </main>

    <footer>
        <p>© 2026 My Website</p>
    </footer>

</body>
```

The structure itself tells us what each part means.

---

# 12. What Is Accessibility?

Accessibility means:

> **Making websites usable by as many people as possible, including people with disabilities.**

For example, some users may use:

- Screen readers
- Keyboard navigation
- Voice controls
- Magnification tools
- Other assistive technologies

Good HTML makes websites easier for these tools to understand.

---

# 13. Use `<button>` for Buttons

Bad:

```html
<div onclick="submitForm()">
    Submit
</div>
```

Better:

```html
<button onclick="submitForm()">
    Submit
</button>
```

Why?

A real `<button>` already has built-in browser behavior.

It can be:

- Focused with the keyboard
- Activated with keyboard controls
- Recognized as a button by assistive technologies

---

# 14. Use `<a>` for Links

If something takes the user to another page:

```html
<a href="/about">
    About
</a>
```

Don't use a random `<div>` and make it clickable with JavaScript when an actual link is appropriate.

---

# 15. Images and `alt`

Images should generally have alternative text.

```html
<img
    src="profile.jpg"
    alt="Nikitha smiling"
>
```

The `alt` text can help users who cannot see the image understand what it represents.

For a decorative image, the alternative text can be empty:

```html
<img src="decoration.png" alt="">
```

---

# 16. Form Labels

Instead of:

```html
<input type="email">
```

use a label:

```html
<label for="email">
    Email
</label>

<input
    type="email"
    id="email"
>
```

Now the relationship between the label and input is clear.

Clicking the label can also focus the input.

---

# 17. Headings

Headings should describe the structure of the content.

```html
<h1>My Portfolio</h1>

<h2>About Me</h2>

<h2>Projects</h2>

<h3>Expense Tracker</h3>
```

Think of headings like the structure of a document:

```text
H1
 ├── H2
 │    └── H3
 └── H2
      └── H3
```

Don't choose heading levels simply because a particular heading "looks bigger."

CSS should control appearance.

HTML headings should represent structure.

---

# 18. Why Semantic HTML Matters

Semantic HTML helps with:

### Accessibility

Assistive technologies can better understand the page.

### SEO

Search engines can better understand the structure and meaning of content.

### Maintainability

Developers can understand the page structure more easily.

### Browser behavior

Semantic elements often come with meaningful built-in behavior.

---

# 19. Semantic HTML Mental Model

Instead of thinking:

```text
"What element makes this look right?"
```

Think:

```text
"What does this content actually mean?"
```

For example:

```text
Navigation
    ↓
<nav>

Main content
    ↓
<main>

Independent post
    ↓
<article>

Section of content
    ↓
<section>

Button
    ↓
<button>

Link
    ↓
<a>
```

---

# 20. The Main Idea

A good HTML page should communicate its structure even before CSS is added.

Imagine removing all the CSS.

You should still be able to understand:

```text
Header
  ↓
Navigation
  ↓
Main content
  ↓
Sections
  ↓
Articles
  ↓
Footer
```

That is one of the biggest ideas behind semantic HTML.

---

# Key Takeaways

- Semantic HTML uses elements according to their meaning.
- `<header>`, `<nav>`, `<main>`, `<section>`, `<article>`, `<aside>`, and `<footer>` provide meaningful structure.
- Use `<button>` for buttons.
- Use `<a>` for navigation links.
- Use `alt` text appropriately for images.
- Use `<label>` with form inputs.
- Use headings to represent content structure.
- Accessibility means making websites usable by people with different abilities and assistive technologies.
- Semantic HTML improves accessibility, maintainability, and understanding of the page structure.

---

## One-Line Summary

> **Semantic HTML describes what content means, while accessibility ensures that the content and interactions can be understood and used by as many people as possible.**2
